use scaly.memory.Page
use dazzle.Interpreter.Interpreter

; ---- the FRAME MARK ------------------------------------------------------
; Frame-granular reclamation for the DSSSL evaluator. `ProcessContext` brackets a
; whole construction rule in one region; this brackets every VM FRAME inside it
; by MARKING the region's bump pointer on entry and rewinding it on exit. The
; measurements behind the shape are in tests/dazzle/PERFORMANCE.md ("Die Frames
; GEZÄHLT", "Die Marke GEBAUT"): 82 M frames on a heavy codegen at 415 bytes
; each, 99.7 % of them under 4 KB, 4140 frames per eval bracket — so a region per
; frame is unaffordable and unnecessary, while a mark costs a store.
;
; A rewind is only sound if nothing above the mark is reachable afterwards, so
; everything here is the CONSERVATIVE test that decides it. Six conditions, all
; O(1), and a frame is rewound only when every one holds:
;
;   1. VALID     — the mark was taken in the eval bracket that is still current.
;   2. THE CHAIN — `rewind` refuses unless the mark's page is still the
;                  region's end; the pages the frame appended go back with it.
;   3. EXCLUSIVE — the mark page's exclusive-page list has not moved, so no
;                  oversized allocation put a PageNode inside the span
;                  (`rewind` checks this against the value taken at mark
;                  time and changes nothing when it moved).
;   4. RESULT    — the value the frame RETURNS does not lie in the span. This is
;                  the one escape route the frame owns, and it is an address
;                  comparison rather than a graph walk.
;   5. QUIET     — nothing PUBLISHED while the frame was running.
;   6. BACKFILL  — the region HEAD served no allocation while the frame ran.
;                  `Page.allocate` bumps the page it was CALLED ON when that
;                  page still has room, and every `ELObj.make_*` calls it on the
;                  head — so the frontier is not the tail alone. See the block
;                  above `below_sum`; this is the condition the memo seam's three
;                  wrong codegens were pointing at.
;
; ★★★Condition 5 is the whole trick, and it is ONE global counter. A frame's
; lifetime is exactly the window in which only IT and its DESCENDANTS run (a
; sibling runs after the pop), and every ancestor's span CONTAINS this frame's.
; So "no publication happened anywhere between my push and my pop" is sufficient
; in the conservative reading, and needs no propagation to ancestors — just
; `epoch` against `epoch_at_push`.
;
; ★★A publication is a store that could put a pointer into a marked span into
; something that survives it. The audited list: the three `SetBox` variants, a
; control-stack unwind (call/cc), a frame popped without a reported result, and
; the four lazy MEMOISATIONS the hot primitives reach (`force_select`,
; `concat_fill`, the string rope's flatten, the grove's view arrays). `store`
; decides each by TARGET — in another region it can never receive such a pointer,
; and inside the running frame's own span it dies together with it.
;
; ★★★The class that made the audit worth doing: the engine's `Page.get(object)`
; idiom protects BRACKET granularity — object region and caller scratch are
; different regions — but NOT a frame mark, because within ONE region
; `Page.get(x)` resolves to the region's END, which is above every live mark.
; That holds for every container grown off a PASSED-IN container.
;
; ★★★ON by default since 2026-08-07 — and what that decision rests on, stated
; so nobody has to reconstruct it:
;
;   PROVEN, in the sense that a counter-example would have shown up:
;     * conditions 1-4 are local arguments about addresses this frame owns;
;     * condition 6's O(1) form was cross-checked against its GENERAL form
;       (sum over every page below the mark, `DZ_FRAME_MARK_BACKFILL=N`, 27x
;       slower) with ZERO missed cases: 260 860 rewinds exhaustively on one
;       codegen and 4105 strided on the 1804-file one;
;     * differentially: 48 real-world codegens, 469 ESIS models, three backends
;       on the DocBook corpus and every suite, in BOTH engine modes.
;
;   NOT PROVEN, and this is the residual risk: condition 5's completeness. It
;   rests on a SWEEP over the engine's store sites (all 29 `Page.get(` sites
;   plus the mutating calls a primitive can reach). A store nobody hooked is
;   invisible to every gate until an output differs, and this class HAS bitten
;   once — the memo seam, where the fault turned out to be the allocator
;   (condition 6) rather than a store. A runtime scan cannot close it either:
;   a constantly rewound region is full of dead words no scan can tell from
;   live pointers, so the OUTPUT is the only oracle. The proof belongs in the
;   compiler, as the escape analysis of ROADMAP-dazzle.md's "Memory model" —
;   which exists now (dazzle/Escape.scaly) but proves 3.6 % of the bytes
;   frame-local against this mark's 38 %, so it cannot stand in for it.
;
; ⇒ The escape hatch is therefore load-bearing, not decoration:
; `DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK=0` or `--no-frame-mark` turns the mark off (and with it the
; memo seam, which follows it). If a tree ever comes out wrong, that switch is
; both the workaround and the first half of the diagnosis; `DZ_FRAME_MARK_LIMIT`
; then bisects to the single rewind that did it. tests/dazzle/framemark/run.sh
; gates the OFF path, because ON is now what every other suite runs.
; Measured on the heaviest codegen, with the memo seam that follows the mark
; (Interpreter.memo_host): peak footprint 5968 -> 3463 MB (-42.0 %), max RSS
; 5310 -> 3154 MB (-40.6 %), 17.22 GB reclaimed, cpu -0.6 % (noise).
;
; ★What was tried and MEASURED AWAY — do not rebuild it: a second arena for
; frame-local values with the escaping result placed in the eval region. Sound
; only if no argument of the escaping call lives in the frame arena, and that
; collapses, because a value produced by a non-tail call taints every consumer:
; 13 754 of 14 028 tail calls stayed behind and the win went negative. Breaking
; that cycle needs to know at the PRODUCER whether its value escapes, which is
; the dataflow and not a runtime test ("Sprosse 3 GEBAUT und VERWORFEN").
;
; ★★★And a SECOND one, measured away on 2026-08-07 — do not rebuild this either:
; a granularity heuristic that skips the mark where it does not pay ("mark only
; from the Nth frame of an eval bracket", `DZ_FRAME_MARK_MIN`, built and
; swept). It is perfectly SOUND — not marking a frame can only cost yield — and
; it is still worthless, because it treats the wrong half of the cost:
;
;   styling, 1.06 M frames | instructions | reclaimed
;   mark off               |     7.004 G  |        —
;   mark on, NOTHING marked|     7.078 G  |      0 B     <- the HOOK alone
;   mark on                |     7.091 G  |   9.56 MB
;
; ★★★So 85 % of what the mark costs that workload is being CALLED — the wrapper,
; the call, the entry, the pop's condition 1 — and only 15 % is the mark. On the
; heaviest codegen the same experiment says 70 % hook / 30 % mark (5.24 of
; 7.48 G), and there the marking half buys 14.25 GB, i.e. 6.4 bytes per
; instruction. The best threshold found recovered 3 % of the styling cost while
; giving up 77 % of the yield, and the knob cost 0.13 % just by being asked.
; ⇒ Whoever wants this back has to move the decision to the CALL SITE, and then
; owes a proof that push and pop can never disagree about it: a frame whose push
; skipped and whose pop did not reads a STALE column, and a stale column with a
; live generation is a rewind to a bogus mark. That is the corruption class this
; whole file is written to avoid, for 1 % of one workload.
;
; ---- the SHAPE of this file, which is a measurement ----------------------
;
; ★★★The mechanism costs 173 instructions per frame on the heaviest codegen
; (14.19 G for 81 775 653 frames) where its design says "one store". The
; decomposition that explains the gap is in PERFORMANCE.md ("Die Marke
; ZERLEGT"), and three of its findings are BUILT INTO the code below — read
; them before "simplifying" any of it back:
;
;   1. ONE STATE BLOCK, not eighteen globals. AArch64 materialises an `adrp`
;      per GLOBAL, not per 4 KB page: every one of these globals lives in the
;      same page and the hot path still paid ~25 `adrp` per frame. So the whole
;      hot state — the scalars AND the per-frame columns — sits in one calloc'd
;      block behind `fm_s`, read once into a register (`let f fm_s`) and
;      addressed by the FM_*/FC_* word offsets below. A new hot field goes in
;      the block; a new instrument switch stays a global, because it is only
;      read on the probe path.
;   2. The per-frame columns are ONE 64-byte line per frame (FM_COLS = 8), not
;      seven parallel arrays. Same instruction count for the stores, one cache
;      line instead of seven.
;   3. ONE PRE-COMBINED PROBE WORD (`FM_PROBE`), the same trick as
;      `Escape.es_stats_on`: stats, trap, trap-pop, scan, verify, backfill and
;      limit are all OFF in real-world, and asking for them one at a time cost
;      26 of the 182 instructions. `setup` publishes FM_PROBE LAST, so the hot
;      path can never see the summary before the switches it stands for. The
;      counters live behind it too — which is why every instrument has to be
;      re-run and compared LINE BY LINE after touching this file: a probe word
;      can switch a measurement off silently and the output stays identical.
;   4. The printers are OUT of the hot functions (`trap_print`, shared by the
;      push and the pop trap so the inliner cannot pull it back into either).
;      They were the reason `push_on` opened with six `stp` and closed with six
;      `ldp` — 13 instructions per frame for code that never runs. Check with
;      one line of disassembler after any change here:
;         objdump -d <bin> | sed -n '/<__ZN9FrameMark7push_onEi>:/,/ret/p'
;      No `stp` in the hot prologue, and the only `bl` on a cold branch.

function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern
function calloc(count: size_t, size: size_t) returns pointer[void] extern
procedure atexit(cb: pointer[void]) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eputs(s: pointer[const_char]) extern
; SIGTRAP, so a debugger stops exactly at the push named by DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP
procedure raise(sig: i32) returns i32 extern
procedure scaly_eputi(value: i64) extern
procedure scaly_eputnl() extern

; deeper frames are counted, not marked
define FM_MAX_DEPTH: i64 65536

; --- the state block, by word offset --------------------------------------
; scalars first, then FM_MAX_DEPTH frames of FM_COLS words each
define FM_HEAD: i64 0       ; the region being marked (the eval bracket's)
define FM_GEN: i64 1        ; its generation — condition 1
define FM_EPOCH: i64 2      ; the publication counter — condition 5
define FM_TOP: i64 3        ; the innermost live frame's index
define FM_PROBE: i64 4      ; any instrument on? see finding 3 above
define FM_FRAMES: i64 5
define FM_REWOUND: i64 6
define FM_BYTES: i64 7
define FM_PAGES: i64 8
define FM_PUBLISHES: i64 9
define FM_BINVALID: i64 10
define FM_BPUBLISH: i64 11
define FM_BRESULT: i64 12
define FM_BREFUSED: i64 13
define FM_BDEEP: i64 14
define FM_BBELOW: i64 15
define FM_HDR: i64 32       ; where the per-frame columns start

; one 64-byte line per frame
define FM_COLS: i64 8
define FC_PAGE: i64 0       ; the mark's page
define FC_OFF: i64 1        ; the mark itself
define FC_EXCL: i64 2       ; the mark page's exclusive list — condition 3
define FC_HEXCL: i64 3      ; the region HEAD's, a DIFFERENT list
define FC_HEADAT: i64 4     ; the head's bump at the push — condition 6
define FC_GEN: i64 5        ; -1 means "this frame carries no mark"
define FC_EPOCH: i64 6

; 0 = not asked yet, 1 = on, 2 = off. Its own global: the thin wrappers below
; are inlined into their callers, where the block pointer is not live.
shared fm_state: int 0
shared fm_s: pointer[i64] null

; --- the instrument switches, read on the PROBE path only ------------------
shared fm_stats: bool false
; probe-only side tables: the trap's sequence numbers and the general form of
; condition 6. Both are per frame and neither is touched in real-world.
shared fm_seq: pointer[i64] null
shared fm_below: pointer[i64] null

; --- the HUNT for the store the sweep does not hook (DZ_FRAME_MARK_LIMIT/SCAN) -
;
; The memo-host probe (`DZ_MEMO_PERM=1`, Interpreter.memo_host) removes 17.5 M
; publications and makes 3 of the 48 real-world codegens write garbage — so a
; publication nobody meant as protection was covering a store this file never
; hooked. These two switches are how that store gets found instead of guessed:
;
;   DZ_FRAME_MARK_LIMIT=N  perform only the first N rewinds. The run with limit N
;                          is IDENTICAL to the unlimited run up to its Nth rewind
;                          (the prefix is deterministic), so a binary search on N
;                          converges on the exact rewind that corrupts.
;   DZ_FRAME_MARK_SCAN=N   at rewind N, before releasing anything, scan the
;                          marked region and the run arena for a word pointing
;                          INTO the span about to be reclaimed — that word is the
;                          referrer, and its region says which store wrote it.
shared fm_limit: i64 0 - 1
shared fm_scan: i64 0 - 1
; DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP=<seq>: SIGTRAP at the PUSH of the frame with that sequence
; number, having printed its mark page. That is the only moment at which a
; debugger can watch the word a later store corrupts — at the pop it is too
; late, and the arena base moves between runs, so an address from a previous run
; is worthless. The pop prints the sequence number, the push traps on it.
;
; ★★DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP_POP=<seq> is the OTHER half, and without it a watchpoint
; proves nothing: the word below a mark is recycled after every rewind, so a hit
; only implicates the frame under investigation when it falls inside ITS window.
; The push trap opens the window, this one closes it, and the last hit in
; between is the writer. DZ_FRAME_MARK_WATCH=<bytes> makes the push trap print
; the address that many bytes BELOW the mark — the referrer the SCAN found,
; computed in THIS run, because the arena base moves between runs.
shared fm_trap: i64 0 - 1
shared fm_trap_pop: i64 0 - 1
shared fm_watch: i64 1040
; the push trap parks the computed address here so a debugger can read it out of
; a SYMBOL instead of scraping stderr — the instrument globals carry no debug
; info, but they are symbols, and the arena base moves between runs. ★It stays
; its OWN global for exactly that reason: a word inside the block has no name.
shared fm_watch_at: i64 0
; ★★★DZ_FRAME_MARK_VERIFY=1 — the check the store sweep never had. Before EVERY
; rewind, scan the marked region for a word below the mark that points into the
; span about to be reclaimed. A hit is a rewind that frees live data, i.e. a
; store the sweep does not hook. The marked region is two pages on this
; workload, so the scan is cheap enough to run over a whole codegen — which
; turns "empirically gated" into "checked", and answers the question that
; matters: does the DEFAULT configuration have violations too?
shared fm_verify: bool false
shared fm_violations: i64 0
shared fm_verified: i64 0
; DZ_FRAME_MARK_BACKFILL=N — run the general form of condition 6 on every Nth
; rewind. N=1 is exhaustive and costs 27x on the reproducer; a stride is what
; makes the heaviest codegen (81.8 M frames) reachable at all.
shared fm_backfill: i64 0
shared fm_below_missed: i64 0
shared fm_below_checked: i64 0
shared fm_walk: i64 0
shared fm_walk_max: i64 0

; the atexit entry point: a bare module-level function name is a function
; pointer value, which a concept static is not.
function fm_stats_atexit()
    FrameMark.report()

define FrameMark
{
    procedure on() returns bool
    {
        if fm_state = 0
            FrameMark.setup()
        fm_state = 1
    }

    ; ★ON by default since 2026-08-07; `DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK=0` (or the CLI's
    ; `--no-frame-mark`) is the OPT-OUT, and it is meant to be used: if a tree
    ; ever comes out wrong, that switch is the answer, and the difference it
    ; makes is the diagnosis. Anything else, including the variable being unset,
    ; turns the mark on. `FrameMark.force_off` short-circuits this whole
    ; procedure and must therefore run before the first frame push.
    procedure setup()
    {
        set fm_state: 2
        let e getenv("DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK")
        if e <> null
        {
            let zero "0" as char
            if (*e as u32) = 0
                return
            if (*e as u32) = (zero as u32)
                return
        }
        ; ONE block for the whole hot state — see finding 1 in the header.
        set fm_s: calloc((FM_HDR + (FM_MAX_DEPTH * FM_COLS)) as size_t, 8) as pointer[i64]
        set fm_seq: calloc(FM_MAX_DEPTH as size_t, 8) as pointer[i64]
        set fm_below: calloc(FM_MAX_DEPTH as size_t, 8) as pointer[i64]
        if fm_s = null
            return
        if fm_seq = null
            return
        if fm_below = null
            return
        let lim getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_LIMIT")
        if lim <> null
            set fm_limit: FrameMark.parse_i64(lim)
        let sc getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_SCAN")
        if sc <> null
            set fm_scan: FrameMark.parse_i64(sc)
        let tr getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP")
        if tr <> null
            set fm_trap: FrameMark.parse_i64(tr)
        let tp getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP_POP")
        if tp <> null
            set fm_trap_pop: FrameMark.parse_i64(tp)
        let wa getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_WATCH")
        if wa <> null
            set fm_watch: FrameMark.parse_i64(wa)
        let vf getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_VERIFY")
        if vf <> null
            set fm_verify: true
        let bf getenv("DZ_FRAME_MARK_BACKFILL")
        if bf <> null
            set fm_backfill: FrameMark.parse_i64(bf)
        let s getenv("DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK_STATS")
        if s <> null
        {
            set fm_stats: true
            atexit(&fm_stats_atexit)
        }
        ; ★★★The probe word, published LAST so that it can never be true before
        ; the switch it summarises is set — same discipline as
        ; `Escape.es_stats_on`. Every instrument, INCLUDING the counters, hangs
        ; off it; a new switch must be added here or it will silently never fire.
        var probe: i64 0
        if fm_stats
            set probe: 1
        if fm_verify
            set probe: 1
        if fm_backfill > 0
            set probe: 1
        if fm_limit >= 0
            set probe: 1
        if fm_scan >= 0
            set probe: 1
        if fm_trap >= 0
            set probe: 1
        if fm_trap_pop >= 0
            set probe: 1
        let f fm_s
        set *(f + FM_PROBE): probe
        set fm_state: 1
    }

    ; The CLI's `--no-frame-mark`. Setting the state to "off" before anything
    ; asks makes `on()` skip `setup` entirely, which is why this is a state
    ; write and not a flag `setup` would read: it has to beat the lazy init.
    procedure force_off()
        set fm_state: 2

    function parse_i64(t: pointer[const_char]) returns i64
    {
        var v: i64 0
        var p t as pointer[u8]
        let zero "0" as char
        while (*p) <> 0
        {
            let d (*p as i64) - (zero as i64)
            if d < 0
                return v
            if d > 9
                return v
            set v: (v * 10) + d
            set p: p + 1
        }
        v
    }

    ; --- the region, read and written by documented OFFSET ------------------
    ;
    ; ★★★Why by offset and not through a `Page.mark`/`Page.rewind` pair in the
    ; stdlib, which is where this knowledge belongs: it was TRIED and it fails a
    ; structural constraint of the in-process JIT. The planner is DEMAND-DRIVEN,
    ; so a stdlib function nothing in the COMPILER calls is never emitted into
    ; the compiler image (adding the six functions left `tools/cycle.sh`
    ; IDENTICAL, which is the same fact seen from the other side) — and the
    ; `--jit` leg of tests/dazzle/run.sh materialises this package INSIDE the
    ; compiler process and resolves its externals there. Result:
    ; `Failed to materialize symbols: { _ZN4Page6rewindE… }` and the whole leg
    ; red. **A new stdlib entry point may only be called from a package that the
    ; --jit leg exercises if the compiler itself also reaches it.** So the four
    ; slots are read here, against the comment in `Page.scaly` that declares them
    ; load-bearing by number:
    ;
    ;   0 next_object   1 current_page   2 next_page   3 exclusive_pages.head
    ;
    ; `Page.get` and `Page.release_page` ARE used by the compiler and therefore
    ; resolve — those two stay ordinary calls.

    function slot(p: i64, i: int) returns i64
        *((p as pointer[i64]) + i)

    ; the END of the marked region's extension chain
    function tail(head: i64) returns i64
    {
        let cp FrameMark.slot(head, 1)
        if cp = 0
            return head
        cp
    }

    ; --- hooks --------------------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; ★Each hook is a THIN wrapper around its `*_on` core, and that shape is
    ; load-bearing for the OFF path (the escape hatch). The check used to sit at
    ; the top of the big core, so every frame push, every frame pop and every
    ; store site paid a CALL to find out that the mechanism is off — measured
    ; 2026-08-07 at 2.0 % of a codegen run (`FrameMark::push` 1.10 %,
    ; `::pop` 0.80 %, `::store` 0.09 % of self time, with the mark OFF). Split
    ; this way, LTO inlines the wrapper into its callers and the off path is a
    ; load of `fm_state` plus a branch. Same class as the two switches that
    ; `VM.eval` reads once before its loop.

    ; VM.push_frame, after csp was incremented: the new frame's index is csp-1
    procedure push(csp: int)
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.push_on(csp)
    }

    procedure pop(csp: int, result: pointer[void])
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.pop_on(csp, result)
    }

    procedure pop_unknown()
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    procedure store(target: pointer[void])
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.store_on(target)
    }

    procedure publish()
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    procedure push_on(csp: int)
    {
        let f fm_s
        let idx (csp - 1) as i64
        set *(f + FM_TOP): idx
        let probe *(f + FM_PROBE)
        ; the frame COUNTER is an instrument too: it is the trap's sequence
        ; number and the stats line's first column, and nothing else reads it.
        if probe <> 0
            FrameMark.push_probe(f, idx)
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
            return
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (idx * FM_COLS))
        if head = 0
        {
            ; outside any eval bracket the run page is the host, and that is
            ; never rewound
            set *(c + FC_GEN): 0 - 1
            return
        }
        let t FrameMark.tail(head)
        let b FrameMark.slot(t, 0)
        if b = 0
        {
            set *(c + FC_GEN): 0 - 1
            if probe <> 0
                FrameMark.push_tail(f, idx, t, b)
            return
        }
        set *(c + FC_PAGE): t
        set *(c + FC_OFF): b
        set *(c + FC_EXCL): FrameMark.slot(t, 3)
        set *(c + FC_HEXCL): FrameMark.slot(head, 3)
        set *(c + FC_HEADAT): FrameMark.slot(head, 0)
        set *(c + FC_GEN): *(f + FM_GEN)
        set *(c + FC_EPOCH): *(f + FM_EPOCH)
        ; ★LAST, and that position is load-bearing: in TAIL position this is a
        ; sibling call, so `push_on` needs no stack frame at all — with the trap
        ; sitting in the middle (where the pre-rewrite code had it) the register
        ; allocator spilled five callee-saved PAIRS on every one of 81.8 M
        ; pushes for a branch that never runs. The price is that the push trap
        ; now fires with the frame's columns already written, which is if
        ; anything the better moment to inspect them.
        if probe <> 0
            FrameMark.push_tail(f, idx, t, b)
    }

    ; --- the probe path: everything that is off in real-world ---------------

    procedure push_probe(mutable f: pointer[i64], idx: i64)
    {
        set *(f + FM_FRAMES): *(f + FM_FRAMES) + 1
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
        {
            set *(f + FM_BDEEP): *(f + FM_BDEEP) + 1
            return
        }
        set *(fm_seq + idx): *(f + FM_FRAMES)
    }

    ; the trap, plus the general verifier's push-side snapshot. ★The verifier
    ; samples by FRAME so that the walk is strided on the push side too — at the
    ; push there is no rewind number yet, and walking on every push is what
    ; makes stride 1 unaffordable on a workload with 81.8 M frames.
    ; ★The TRAP goes last inside here, and that is the same reason as above one
    ; level down: this procedure is small enough that LTO inlines it back into
    ; `push_on`, so `trap_print` is only a sibling call — and `push_on` only
    ; frameless — when nothing follows it.
    procedure push_tail(f: pointer[i64], idx: i64, t: i64, b: i64)
    {
        if b <> 0
        {
            if fm_backfill > 0
            {
                set *(fm_below + idx): 0 - 1
                if (*(f + FM_FRAMES) % fm_backfill) = 0
                    set *(fm_below + idx): FrameMark.below_sum(*(f + FM_HEAD), t)
            }
        }
        if fm_trap < 0
            return
        if *(f + FM_FRAMES) <> fm_trap
            return
        FrameMark.trap_print("push", *(f + FM_FRAMES), t, b, true)
    }

    ; ★Shared by BOTH traps on purpose. A printer called from one place is what
    ; the inliner pulls back into the hot function — and that is where
    ; `push_on`'s six `stp` came from.
    procedure trap_print(what: Slice[char], seq: i64, page: i64, mark: i64, watch: bool)
    {
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: TRAP at "
        write(2, what.data as pointer[void], what.length)
        scaly_eputs " of frame seq "
        scaly_eputi seq
        if watch
        {
            scaly_eputs ", mark page "
            scaly_eputi page
            scaly_eputs ", mark "
            scaly_eputi mark
            scaly_eputs ", WATCH "
            scaly_eputi(mark - fm_watch)
            set fm_watch_at: mark - fm_watch
        }
        scaly_eputnl()
        raise(5)
    }

    ; The ReturnInsn arm and PrimitiveObj::tailCall, before VM.pop_frame — `csp`
    ; is still the leaving frame's, so its index is csp-1, and `result` is the
    ; value it yields. ★89 % of frames leave through the tail-call path, not
    ; through the Return insn: a DSSSL body typically ends in a primitive call in
    ; tail position, and treating that path as "no result reported" makes this
    ; whole mechanism report zero.
    procedure pop_on(csp: int, result: pointer[void])
    {
        let f fm_s
        let idx (csp - 1) as i64
        set *(f + FM_TOP): idx - 1
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
            return
        let probe *(f + FM_PROBE)
        ; the closing half of the watch window — see DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP_POP
        if probe <> 0
            FrameMark.pop_trap(idx)
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        if head = 0
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_BINVALID): *(f + FM_BINVALID) + 1
            return
        }
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (idx * FM_COLS))
        ; 1. VALID
        if *(c + FC_GEN) <> *(f + FM_GEN)
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_BINVALID): *(f + FM_BINVALID) + 1
            return
        }
        ; 5. QUIET — cheapest and the most common no
        if *(c + FC_EPOCH) <> *(f + FM_EPOCH)
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_BPUBLISH): *(f + FM_BPUBLISH) + 1
            return
        }
        ; 4. RESULT
        if FrameMark.in_span(c, head, result)
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_BRESULT): *(f + FM_BRESULT) + 1
            return
        }
        ; 6. BACKFILL — the region head served nothing while the frame ran.
        ; ★Only when the head is NOT the mark's page: on a single-page region the
        ; head's bump IS the span's, so testing it there refuses everything
        ; (measured: 92 refusals instead of 3, and the 89 are all this case).
        if *(c + FC_PAGE) <> head
        {
            if FrameMark.slot(head, 0) <> *(c + FC_HEADAT)
            {
                if probe <> 0
                    set *(f + FM_BBELOW): *(f + FM_BBELOW) + 1
                return
            }
        }
        ; the hunt's switches, the general form of condition 6 and the verifier
        if probe <> 0
        {
            if FrameMark.pop_probe(f, idx, c) = false
                return
        }
        ; 2. THE CHAIN and 3. EXCLUSIVE both live in `rewind`, which changes
        ; nothing when it cannot honour the mark.
        if FrameMark.rewind(f, c, head)
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_REWOUND): *(f + FM_REWOUND) + 1
        }
        else
        {
            if probe <> 0
                set *(f + FM_BREFUSED): *(f + FM_BREFUSED) + 1
        }
    }

    procedure pop_trap(idx: i64)
    {
        if fm_trap_pop < 0
            return
        if *(fm_seq + idx) <> fm_trap_pop
            return
        FrameMark.trap_print("pop", fm_trap_pop, 0, 0, false)
    }

    ; answers false when this rewind must NOT happen
    procedure pop_probe(mutable f: pointer[i64], idx: i64, c: pointer[i64]) returns bool
    {
        ; the general form of condition 6, opt-in: every page below the mark.
        ; Counts what the head test alone would let through — see below_sum.
        if fm_backfill > 0
        {
            let snap *(fm_below + idx)
            if snap <> (0 - 1)
            {
                set fm_below_checked: fm_below_checked + 1
                if FrameMark.below_sum(*(f + FM_HEAD), *(c + FC_PAGE)) <> snap
                {
                    set fm_below_missed: fm_below_missed + 1
                    return false
                }
            }
        }
        if fm_limit >= 0
        {
            if *(f + FM_REWOUND) >= fm_limit
            {
                set *(f + FM_BREFUSED): *(f + FM_BREFUSED) + 1
                return false
            }
        }
        if fm_scan >= 0
        {
            if *(f + FM_REWOUND) = fm_scan
            {
                let mp *(c + FC_PAGE)
                scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN frame seq "
                scaly_eputi(*(fm_seq + idx))
                scaly_eputs ", mark page "
                scaly_eputi mp
                scaly_eputnl()
                FrameMark.scan_for_refs(*(f + FM_HEAD), *(c + FC_OFF), FrameMark.slot(mp, 0))
            }
        }
        if fm_verify
        {
            let mp2 *(c + FC_PAGE)
            set fm_verified: fm_verified + 1
            if FrameMark.count_refs(*(f + FM_HEAD), *(c + FC_OFF), FrameMark.slot(mp2, 0)) > 0
                set fm_violations: fm_violations + 1
        }
        true
    }

    ; --- condition 6: BACKFILL ------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; ★★★The store the sweep never hooked was not a store at all — it is the
    ; ALLOCATOR. `Page.allocate` serves from the page it was CALLED ON whenever
    ; that page still has room (`capacity < size` is what sends a request to the
    ; region's tail), and the engine calls it on the region HEAD (`host.allocate`
    ; in every `ELObj.make_*`) and on arbitrary pages of the chain (the
    ; `Page.get(object)` / `^this` / container-growth idiom). So the frontier is
    ; NOT the tail alone: a page below the mark that kept a few free bytes serves
    ; small requests forever, BELOW every live mark.
    ;
    ; That breaks the span's defining property. Measured on the reproducer:
    ; `ELObj.make_string` got its 40-byte ELObj from the head's 56-byte remainder
    ; — below the mark — and the string buffer it then allocates no longer fitted
    ; there, so it came off the tail, ABOVE the mark. The rewind freed the buffer
    ; and left the object holding a pointer into it: 150 characters of generated
    ; output turned into garbage bytes in one file of 278. Found with
    ; DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP + DZ_FRAME_MARK_TRAP_POP and a watchpoint on the word the
    ; SCAN named, which is the only way to attribute a write to a frame's window
    ; — the word is recycled after every rewind.
    ;
    ; The test that ships is ONE comparison: the region HEAD's bump pointer must
    ; be the same at the pop as it was at the push. Bump pointers only move
    ; forward and no page before the mark's is released while the frame runs, so
    ; equality means the head served nothing during the window. No propagation is
    ; needed — an ancestor's window contains its descendants', exactly as for
    ; condition 5.
    ;
    ; ★★★Why the HEAD and not every page below the mark: `this` is the head for
    ; every allocation the engine makes from a page it did not derive from an
    ; object (`host.allocate`, and `host` IS `interp.get_host()` = the eval
    ; region head). A MIDDLE page can only become `this` through
    ; `Page.get(object)` on a PRE-EXISTING object — and that is precisely the set
    ; of sites the store sweep hooks, so such an allocation already publishes and
    ; is stopped by condition 5. Head test plus store sweep is the whole rule.
    ;
    ; ★★And the general test was MEASURED AWAY rather than assumed away, because
    ; the first version of this condition was the general one: sum the bumps of
    ; every page below the mark. It is correct and it costs **27x** — 2.9 billion
    ; page steps on the 278-file reproducer, because the eval region is not short
    ; (chains of 13 536 pages below a mark with the memo probe, 20 324 without).
    ; The run went 2.18 s -> 59.6 s. It survives as the VERIFIER
    ; `DZ_FRAME_MARK_BACKFILL=1`, which counts the rewinds the general test would
    ; refuse ON TOP of the head test — that number is what gates the paragraph
    ; above, and it is 0 across the corpus.
    procedure below_sum(head: i64, mp: i64) returns i64
    {
        var s: i64 0
        var n: i64 0
        var p head
        while p <> 0
        {
            if p = mp
                break
            set s: s + FrameMark.slot(p, 0)
            set n: n + 1
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        set fm_walk: fm_walk + n
        if n > fm_walk_max
            set fm_walk_max: n
        s
    }

    ; is `v` inside the span the frame at `c` marked — its page from the mark
    ; onward, plus every page appended after it?
    function in_span(c: pointer[i64], head: i64, v: pointer[void]) returns bool
    {
        if v = null
            return false
        let mp *(c + FC_PAGE)
        let vp Page.get(v) as i64
        if vp = mp
        {
            if (v as i64) >= *(c + FC_OFF)
                return true
            return false
        }
        ; another region can never be in the span: a container growing off an
        ; object there allocates at THAT region's end
        if FrameMark.slot(vp, 4) <> head
            return false
        ; same region — only the pages AFTER the mark's page are in the span, and
        ; a frame appends none in 99.7 % of cases, so this walk is usually empty
        var p FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        while p <> 0
        {
            if p = vp
                return true
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        false
    }

    ; Give back everything allocated in the marked region since the frame's mark.
    ; Answers false and changes NOTHING when the mark cannot be honoured.
    ;
    ; Two passes on purpose: VALIDATE the chain first, then release. A page whose
    ; own exclusive-page list grew since the mark must not be dropped half way,
    ; and `allocate_oversized` attaches to the page it was CALLED on rather than
    ; to the head, so the check is per page.
    procedure rewind(mutable f: pointer[i64], c: pointer[i64], head: i64) returns bool
    {
        let mp *(c + FC_PAGE)
        let mark *(c + FC_OFF)
        ; NO "the mark's page must still be the end" test — that was the
        ; single-page restriction, and dropping it is the point: pages appended
        ; after `mp` are exactly what this frame added, because `allocate` only
        ; ever appends at the region's end.
        ; an oversized page has no bump pointer and cannot carry a mark
        let now FrameMark.slot(mp, 0)
        if now = 0
            return false
        if mark < mp
            return false
        if mark > now
            return false
        ; the mark page's own exclusive list must not have moved: an oversized
        ; allocation since the mark put its PageNode inside the span, so the
        ; surviving list head would dangle
        if FrameMark.slot(mp, 3) <> *(c + FC_EXCL)
            return false
        ; ★★And the region HEAD's list as well, which is a DIFFERENT list as soon
        ; as the mark sits on an extension page. `allocate` forwards an oversized
        ; request to `allocate_oversized` on the page it was CALLED on, and a
        ; frame allocates on its host — the head. Its PageNode is then
        ; bump-allocated at the region's end, i.e. inside the span, so the
        ; surviving head's list would dangle. Leaving this out cost 3 of the 48
        ; real-world codegens; the gate is what found it.
        if FrameMark.slot(head, 3) <> *(c + FC_HEXCL)
            return false
        ; the pages this frame APPENDED go back too. It is not the frame's size
        ; that makes that necessary — 99.7 % allocate under 4 KB — but where the
        ; bump pointer happened to stand at entry; measured, it decides 19 % of
        ; all frames. VALIDATE the whole chain first: a page whose own exclusive
        ; list is non-empty must not be dropped (its PageNode sits in the span we
        ; are about to reclaim), and noticing that half way through the release
        ; would leave the region in pieces.
        var p FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        var extra: i64 0
        var pages: i64 0
        while p <> 0
        {
            if FrameMark.slot(p, 3) <> 0
                return false
            set extra: extra + (FrameMark.slot(p, 0) - p)
            set pages: pages + 1
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        if now = mark
        {
            if pages = 0
                return true
        }
        var q FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        while q <> 0
        {
            let following FrameMark.slot(q, 2)
            Page.release_page(q as pointer[Page])
            set q: following
        }
        let w mp as pointer[i64]
        ; cut the chain and make the mark's page the end again. The head records
        ; the end, and null means "the head IS the end" — the state `allocate`
        ; expects, it appends on demand.
        set *(w + 2): 0
        let hw head as pointer[i64]
        if mp = head
            set *(hw + 1): 0
        else
            set *(hw + 1): mp
        set *w: mark
        if *(f + FM_PROBE) <> 0
        {
            set *(f + FM_BYTES): *(f + FM_BYTES) + (now - mark) + extra
            set *(f + FM_PAGES): *(f + FM_PAGES) + pages
        }
        true
    }

    procedure publish_on()
    {
        let f fm_s
        set *(f + FM_EPOCH): *(f + FM_EPOCH) + 1
        if *(f + FM_PROBE) <> 0
            set *(f + FM_PUBLISHES): *(f + FM_PUBLISHES) + 1
    }

    ; ★A STORE into a pre-existing object. It publishes in exactly one shape:
    ; the target lives in the region being marked AND below the innermost frame's
    ; mark — then whatever the store allocates lands at the region's END, inside
    ; the span, while the target survives the rewind. A target in another region
    ; (the grove, the run page) allocates at THAT region's end and can never
    ; point into the span; a target inside the span dies with it.
    procedure store_on(target: pointer[void])
    {
        if target = null
            return
        let f fm_s
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        if head = 0
            return
        let tp Page.get(target) as i64
        if FrameMark.slot(tp, 4) <> head
            return
        let top *(f + FM_TOP)
        if top < 0
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        if top >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (top * FM_COLS))
        if *(c + FC_GEN) <> *(f + FM_GEN)
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        ; ★★★Deliberately STRICTER than `in_span`: the target counts as safe only
        ; when it sits on the innermost frame's own MARK PAGE at or above the
        ; mark, and the region has not moved off that page since. Reasoning that
        ; a target on a LATER page of the span is equally safe is what the
        ; rewritten version did — and it made 3 of the 48 real-world codegens
        ; differ while publishing 42 000 times less. The looser rule may well be
        ; defensible; it is not GATED, and this one is.
        let t FrameMark.tail(head)
        if t <> *(c + FC_PAGE)
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        let r target as i64
        if r >= *(c + FC_OFF)
        {
            if r < FrameMark.slot(t, 0)
                return
        }
        FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    ; Interpreter.set_eval_region — the region under every live mark changes
    procedure region(p: ref[Page]?)
    {
        if FrameMark.on() = false
            return
        let f fm_s
        if (p as i64) = *(f + FM_HEAD)
            return
        set *(f + FM_HEAD): p as i64
        set *(f + FM_GEN): *(f + FM_GEN) + 1
    }

    ; --- the referrer scan (DZ_FRAME_MARK_SCAN / _VERIFY) -------------------
    ;
    ; ★Every 8-byte word of the marked region and of the run arena that points
    ; INTO [lo, hi) — the span this rewind is about to reclaim. A hit in the run
    ; arena means a long-lived object holds it; a hit in the marked region BELOW
    ; the mark means an older frame does. Either way the address identifies the
    ; record, and the record identifies the store.
    procedure scan_for_refs(head: i64, lo: i64, hi: i64)
    {
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN span ["
        scaly_eputi lo
        scaly_eputs ", "
        scaly_eputi hi
        scaly_eputs ") = "
        scaly_eputi(hi - lo)
        scaly_eputs " bytes"
        scaly_eputnl()
        var hits: i64 0
        set hits: hits + FrameMark.scan_region(head, lo, hi, "marked-region")
        let perm Interpreter.perm_page()
        if perm <> null
        {
            if (perm as i64) <> head
                set hits: hits + FrameMark.scan_region(perm as i64, lo, hi, "run-arena")
        }
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN total referrers "
        scaly_eputi hits
        scaly_eputnl()
    }

    ; ★The referrer's identity, printed as OFFSETS (stable across runs — the
    ; arena base is not) plus the neighbourhood of both ends. A record is
    ; recognised by its shape: an `Array` shows {vector, length} with a small
    ; length, a `StringC` shows {data, size}, and the target's bytes as text
    ; usually name the value outright.
    procedure report_ref(what: Slice[char], at: i64, val: i64, page: i64)
    {
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN ref in "
        write(2, what.data as pointer[void], what.length)
        scaly_eputs " at page+"
        scaly_eputi(at - page)
        scaly_eputs " -> page+"
        scaly_eputi(val - page)
        scaly_eputnl()
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN   words around the referrer (it is the 5th):"
        FrameMark.dump_words(at - 64, 17)
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN   words at the target:"
        FrameMark.dump_words(val, 8)
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN   target as text: ["
        FrameMark.dump_text(val, 40)
        scaly_eputs "]"
        scaly_eputnl()
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN   referrer as text: ["
        FrameMark.dump_text(at - 64, 80)
        scaly_eputs "]"
        scaly_eputnl()
    }

    procedure dump_words(from: i64, n: int)
    {
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            scaly_eputs " "
            scaly_eputi(*((from + ((i as i64) * 8)) as pointer[i64]))
            set i: i + 1
        }
        scaly_eputnl()
    }

    ; printable bytes only, so a string inside the record shows up as itself
    procedure dump_text(from: i64, n: int)
    {
        var buf u8[96]
        let b buf as pointer[u8]
        var i 0
        while i < n
        {
            let c *((from + (i as i64)) as pointer[u8])
            var ch c
            if (c as u32) < 32
                set ch: 46 as u8
            if (c as u32) > 126
                set ch: 46 as u8
            set buf[i]: ch
            set i: i + 1
        }
        set buf[n]: 0 as u8
        scaly_eputs(b as pointer[const_char])
    }

    ; the silent form of scan_region, over the marked region only
    function count_refs(head: i64, lo: i64, hi: i64) returns i64
    {
        var hits: i64 0
        var p head
        while p <> 0
        {
            let end FrameMark.slot(p, 0)
            var q p + 40
            while (q + 8) <= end
            {
                var outside true
                if q >= lo
                {
                    if q < hi
                        set outside: false
                }
                if outside
                {
                    let v *(q as pointer[i64])
                    if v >= lo
                    {
                        if v < hi
                            set hits: hits + 1
                    }
                }
                set q: q + 8
            }
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        hits
    }

    function scan_region(head: i64, lo: i64, hi: i64, what: Slice[char]) returns i64
    {
        if head = 0
            return 0
        var hits: i64 0
        var pages: i64 0
        var p head
        while p <> 0
        {
            set pages: pages + 1
            let end FrameMark.slot(p, 0)
            var q p + 40
            while (q + 8) <= end
            {
                ; skip the span itself — it pointing into itself is expected
                var outside true
                if q >= lo
                {
                    if q < hi
                        set outside: false
                }
                if outside
                {
                    let v *(q as pointer[i64])
                    if v >= lo
                    {
                        if v < hi
                        {
                            set hits: hits + 1
                            if hits < 6
                                FrameMark.report_ref(what, q, v, p)
                        }
                    }
                }
                set q: q + 8
            }
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: SCAN "
        write(2, what.data as pointer[void], what.length)
        scaly_eputs ": "
        scaly_eputi pages
        scaly_eputs " pages, "
        scaly_eputi hits
        scaly_eputs " referrers"
        scaly_eputnl()
        hits
    }

    procedure report()
    {
        if fm_stats = false
            return
        let f fm_s
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_FRAMES))
        scaly_eputs " frames, "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_REWOUND))
        scaly_eputs " rewound, "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BYTES))
        scaly_eputs " bytes reclaimed, "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_PAGES))
        scaly_eputs " extension pages handed back"
        scaly_eputnl()
        scaly_eputs "frame-mark: blocked by  publish "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BPUBLISH))
        scaly_eputs "  result "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BRESULT))
        scaly_eputs "  refused "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BREFUSED))
        scaly_eputs "  bracket "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BINVALID))
        scaly_eputs "  too-deep "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BDEEP))
        scaly_eputs "  backfill "
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_BBELOW))
        scaly_eputs "  ("
        scaly_eputi(*(f + FM_PUBLISHES))
        scaly_eputs " publications)"
        scaly_eputnl()
        if fm_backfill > 0
        {
            scaly_eputs "frame-mark: BACKFILL "
            scaly_eputi fm_below_checked
            scaly_eputs " rewinds checked (stride "
            scaly_eputi fm_backfill
            scaly_eputs "), missed by the head test "
            scaly_eputi fm_below_missed
            scaly_eputs ", pages walked "
            scaly_eputi fm_walk
            scaly_eputs ", deepest chain below a mark "
            scaly_eputi fm_walk_max
            scaly_eputnl()
        }
        if fm_verify
        {
            scaly_eputs "frame-mark: VERIFY "
            scaly_eputi fm_verified
            scaly_eputs " rewinds checked, "
            scaly_eputi fm_violations
            scaly_eputs " freed live data"
            scaly_eputnl()
        }
    }
}
